Master Git Like a Pro – Must-Know Commands for Every Developer

Whether you're a beginner starting your DevOps or development journey or an experienced engineer brushing up your Git skills, these essential Git commands will boost your productivity and confidence when working with version control. 

 Here's a quick cheat sheet with examples:
1. git init – Initialize a new Git repository
 2. git clone <repo_url> – Clone a remote repo to your local
 3. git add file.txt – Stage file(s) for commit
 4. git commit -m "message" – Commit your staged changes
 5. git branch branchname – Create a new branch
 6. git checkout branchname – Switch between branches
 7. git merge branchname – Merge a branch into the current one
 8. git push origin branchname – Push changes to remote repo
 9. git pull origin branchname – Pull and merge from remote
 10. git fetch origin – Fetch updates without merging
 11. git stash – Save local changes temporarily
 12. git stash pop – Reapply last stashed changes
 13. git cherry-pick <commit_hash> – Apply a specific commit
 14. git rebase branchname – Reapply commits on a new base
 15. git reflog – View history of reference changes
